# Chapter Five: Shadows, Sisters, and Secrets

The afternoon sun cast long, amber shadows across the manicured lawns of the estate, but inside the quiet guest room, the air felt heavy. Sitting by the edge of the large French window, Ira held her phone tightly. Her fingers hovered over the screen before she finally pressed call, keeping her voice to a tense, urgent whisper once the line connected.

"Vaani... it’s me."

On the other end, a sharp, tearful gasp broke through. *"Ira! Where the hell are you? Everyone’s been looking everywhere, people are saying—"*

"Listen to me, and listen carefully," Ira cut in, her tone rigid to mask the trembling in her voice. "I’m safe. I am completely sound, but I cannot meet you right now. Do *not* look for me, Vaani. Do not ask around. The moment I am ready, I will come to you."

*"Ira, please, let us help you—"*

"No," Ira whispered fiercely, glancing out toward the distant estate gates. "You need to listen to me. Pack a bag, take your things, and leave Manali as soon as possible. Do not stay in this town, Vaani. Please. Just trust me and get out."

Before Vaani could demand answers, Ira disconnected the call, her chest heaving as she pulled her knees to her chest. The walls of the mansion offered sanctuary, but the invisible tendrils of her past were never far behind.
Downstairs in the sprawling, high-security study, the atmosphere was thick with a very different kind of storm.

Shiv sat behind his dark mahogany desk, his jaw clenched tight as he replayed the events of three days prior—the incident that had set a dangerous chain of events into motion.

It had started with a frantic call from the local college. Sindhura and Mandhira, full of fiery spirit and refusing to back down, had been cornered by a group of entitled local boys right outside the campus gates. The harassment had crossed a line, and before anyone could step in, Mandhira had whipped around and landed a vicious, echoing slap across the ringleader's face.

Unfortunately, someone had recorded it. Within seconds, the video had gone viral across local social media channels, weaponizing the Kashyap name and putting an instant target on the twins' backs.

Shiv had gone nuclear. He had returned to the mansion that afternoon in a cold, black fury that had terrified even the seasoned staff. Within hours, he had scrubbed the video from the internet completely and doubled the perimeter security. But Shiv didn't just stop at a digital cleanup. He had deployed KK to track down every single one of those boys to teach them a lesson they would never forget.

A sharp knock at the study door pulled Shiv from his thoughts. KK stepped inside, his expression unusually grim, holding a thick manila folder.

"Sir," KK began, his voice dropping low as he placed the folder on the desk. "We tracked down the boys who cornered the twins. We handled them, made sure they know never to look in the direction of the Kashyap family again."

Shiv leaned back, his cold gray eyes narrowing. "Good. What about the ringleader? The one Mandhira hit?"

KK hesitated for a fraction of a second—a rare sign of unease from a man who had seen it all. "That’s the complication, Sir. We dug deep into his background and financial trail. He’s not just some spoiled local college brat."

Shiv stared at him, the silence in the room turning razor-sharp. "Who is he?"

"His name is Vicky," KK said quietly. "He’s Bagga’s younger brother."

The air in the study instantly froze. Shiv’s gaze locked onto KK, the terrifying realization clicking into place with absolute, chilling clarity.
